{"abstract":"Recent research has begun exploring games as a medium for reflection due to their affordances as interactive systems of challenge. However, little effort has been put into (1) synthesizing insights across studies and disciplines and (2) translating the academic work on reflective play into practical takeaways for game developers. This article takes the first steps toward summarizing existing work on reflective play and translating insights for practical implementation by identifying key game elements present in games that evoke reflection. We divide these elements into five approaches: Disruptions, Slowdowns, Questioning, Revisiting, and Enhancers. Finally, we provide an actionable supplement for practicing game developers to apply these concepts to their games.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Miller, Josh Aaron","Gandhi, Kutub","Whitby, Matthew","Kosa, Mehmet","Cooper, Seth","Mekler, Elisa","Iacovides, Ioanna"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-747250"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Northeastern University","University of York"],"keyword":["Games","Reflection","meaningful games","reflective game design","reflective informatics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a design framework for reflective play","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3613904.3642455","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-747250","identifier":[{"id":"85194836123","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-8fe09eaf-7b24-48f7-9349-26f830c01946"],"keyword":["Games","meaningful games","reflection","reflective game design","reflective informatics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-5373F94FF3D98DD41E50ACEB4FD46A8B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3613904.3642455","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/105519356/chi24a-sub4628-cam-i16.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"northeastern university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Northeastern University","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of york","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of York","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Josh Aaron","key":"millerpau","last":"Miller","name":"Miller, Josh Aaron","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kutub","key":"gandhipau","last":"Gandhi","name":"Gandhi, Kutub","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Matthew","key":"whitbypau","last":"Whitby","name":"Whitby, Matthew","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mehmet","key":"kosapau","last":"Kosa","name":"Kosa, Mehmet","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Seth","key":"cooperpau","last":"Cooper","name":"Cooper, Seth","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Elisa","key":"meklerpau","last":"Mekler","locid":"cf6cfa0b-e3ac-4164-8de2-b956d8e23825","name":"Mekler, Elisa","orcid":"0000-0003-0076-6703","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ioanna","key":"iacovidespau","last":"Iacovides","name":"Iacovides, Ioanna","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3613904.3642455","isbn":["9798400703300"],"status":"p","title":"CHI '24: Proceedings of the CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85194836123"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-8fe09eaf-7b24-48f7-9349-26f830c01946"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A Design Framework for Reflective Play","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-747250","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Motivation and autonomy are fundamental concepts in Human-Computer Interaction (HCI), yet in User Experience (UX) research they have remained surprisingly peripheral. We draw on Self-Determination Theory (SDT) to analyse autonomous and non-autonomous patterns of motivation in 497 interaction experiences. Using latent profile analysis, we identify 5 distinct patterns of motivation in technology use — “motivational profiles” — associated with significant differences in need satisfaction, affect, and usability. Users’ descriptions of these experiences also reveal qualitative differences between profiles: from intentional, purposive engagement, to compulsive use which users themselves consider unhealthy. Our results complicate exclusively positive notions of intrinsic motivation, and clarify how extrinsic motivation can contribute to positive UX. Based on these findings we identify open questions for UX and SDT: addressing “hedonic amotivation” — negative experiences in activities which are intrinsically motivated but not otherwise valued — and “design for internalisation” — scaffolding healthy and sustainable patterns of engagement over time.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Bennett, Daniel","Mekler, Elisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-759418"],"filters":{"added":"2024-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"beyond intrinsic motivation the role of autonomous motivation in user experience","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3689044","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-759418","identifier":[{"id":"85209074208","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-b6fdf1a4-2663-490f-b428-3b8890a13e98"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-FF89C1851445FAEE6F6A1DD5864735A2"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3689044","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3689044","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel","key":"bennettpau","last":"Bennett","name":"Bennett, Daniel","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Elisa","key":"meklerpau","last":"Mekler","locid":"cf6cfa0b-e3ac-4164-8de2-b956d8e23825","name":"Mekler, Elisa","orcid":"0000-0003-0076-6703","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3689044","issn":["10730516","15577325"],"issn_org":["10730516"],"issue":"5","journal":"ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction","volume":"31"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85209074208"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b6fdf1a4-2663-490f-b428-3b8890a13e98"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Beyond Intrinsic Motivation: The Role of Autonomous Motivation in User Experience","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-759418","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Almost a decade ago, Ken Hinckley stressed the importance for HCI program committees and reviewers to champion submitted work that has merit. Rarely, however, are we encouraged to champion published work. In this essay I list five CHI PLAY papers that I like and explain what I like about them. I recommend you do the same.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Mekler, Elisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776441"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Applied computing","HCI theory concepts and models","Human centered computing"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"five chi play papers i like on championing published work that has merit","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3665463.3678868","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Funded by the European Union (ERC, THEORYCRAFT, 101043198)."],"id":"S01-000.00-776441","identifier":[{"id":"85211632857","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-afaf1120-02be-4469-9db9-00ab6d6c6fc8"],"keyword":["Applied computing","HCI theory, concepts and models","Human-centered computing","navelgazing"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B335F84304D26C8E935E37D99227641D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3665463.3678868","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Elisa","key":"meklerpau","last":"Mekler","locid":"cf6cfa0b-e3ac-4164-8de2-b956d8e23825","name":"Mekler, Elisa","orcid":"0000-0003-0076-6703","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3665463.3678868","pages":"279-281","status":"p","title":"CHI PLAY Companion '24: Companion Proceedings of the 2024 Annual Symposium on Computer-Human Interaction in Play"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85211632857"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-afaf1120-02be-4469-9db9-00ab6d6c6fc8"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Five CHI PLAY Papers I Like: On Championing Published Work That Has Merit","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776441","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Thermal-afective experience is a growing topic in Human-Computer Interaction. However, research linking thermal and afective experience in technology use has not moved beyond attempts to establish broad, sweeping associations, such as between warmth and positive afect. One of the obstacles to progression is the need for frameworks and vocabularies that describe and conceptualise the richness of thermal perception and afective experience. To help conceptualise associations between thermal perception and afective experience we turn to Cí poetry, a form of classical Chinese literature rich with evocative descriptions of embodied, environmentally situated, frst-person experience. We conducted a lexical analysis to identify thermal-afective associations, and propose a design framework addressing thermal design. We demonstrate the value of this framework via analysis of existing thermal design exemplars.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Feng, Feng","Bennett, Daniel","Mekler, Elisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-747222"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Cross cultural","Design","Emotion","Framework","Lexicon","Multisensory","Thermal","Translation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"smiles summon the warmth of spring a framework for thermal and affective design grounded in classical chinese poetry","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3643834.3661620","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-747222","identifier":[{"id":"85200394791","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-442081bc-5047-4a4b-be67-b4a24760e2a7"],"keyword":["Thermal","afect","cross-cultural","design","emotion","framework","lexicon","multisensory","translation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-3D6CA289F151D89FE0F8FB4B4CEACE54"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3643834.3661620","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3643834.3661620","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Feng","key":"fengpau","last":"Feng","locid":"dac0e506-da5d-4ee6-8783-5abd2a2096f3","name":"Feng, Feng","orcid":"0000-0002-7127-5437","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel","key":"bennettpau","last":"Bennett","name":"Bennett, Daniel","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Elisa","key":"meklerpau","last":"Mekler","locid":"cf6cfa0b-e3ac-4164-8de2-b956d8e23825","name":"Mekler, Elisa","orcid":"0000-0003-0076-6703","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3643834.3661620","isbn":["9798400705830"],"pages":"2802-2819","status":"p","title":"DIS 2024"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85200394791"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-442081bc-5047-4a4b-be67-b4a24760e2a7"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Smiles Summon the Warmth of Spring: A Framework for Thermal and Affective Design Grounded in Classical Chinese Poetry","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-747222","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Self-determination theory (SDT), a psychological theory of human motivation, is a prominent paradigm in human-computer interaction (HCI) research on games. However, our prior literature review observed a trend towards shallow applications of the theory. This follow-up work takes a broader view – examining SDT scholarship on games, a wider corpus of SDT-based HCI games research (N=259), and perspectives from a games industry practitioner conference – to help explain current applications of SDT. Our findings suggest that perfunctory applications of the theory in HCI games research originate in part from within SDT scholarship on games, which itself exhibits limited engagement with theoretical tenets. Against this backdrop, we unpack the popularity of SDT in HCI games research and identify conditions underlying the theory’s current use as an oft-unquestioned paradigm. Finally, we outline avenues for more productive SDT-informed games research and consider ways towards more intentional practices of theory use in HCI.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Tyack, April","Mekler, Elisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-747235"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Finland"],"glorg":["Aalto University","All Global Affiliations"],"keyword":["Applied computing","Computer games","Empirical studies in HCI","HCI theory","Human centered computing concepts and models"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"self determination theory and hci games research unfulfilled promises and unquestioned paradigms","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3673230","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-747235","identifier":[{"id":"85202916188","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-74f2ce1e-8b3a-4647-8de1-3ee5c40ff953"],"keyword":["Applied computing","Computer games","Empirical studies in HCI","HCI theory","Human-centered computing , concepts and models"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-832CB3EC08F9D243762F832E113832F8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3673230","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3673230","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"fi","key":"aalto university","loc":["Finland","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Aalto University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"April","key":"tyackpau","last":"Tyack","name":"Tyack, April","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Elisa","key":"meklerpau","last":"Mekler","locid":"cf6cfa0b-e3ac-4164-8de2-b956d8e23825","name":"Mekler, Elisa","orcid":"0000-0003-0076-6703","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3673230","issn":["10730516","15577325"],"issn_org":["10730516"],"journal":"ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85202916188"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-74f2ce1e-8b3a-4647-8de1-3ee5c40ff953"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Self-Determination Theory and HCI Games Research: Unfulfilled Promises and Unquestioned Paradigms","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-747235","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Literature reviews (or \"lit reviews\" for short) have become a popular way of knowing in HCI, yet there has been little reflection as to what we want from these reviews and how they can benefit the HCI design research community. Pushes toward more rigor and adopting standards within lit reviews often seem inappropriate and at odds with ways of knowing within the design research community. In this paper, we present critiques and associated risks of these viewpoints, and reflections towards a more thoughtful engagement with literature reviews in HCI design research. We open a discussion of how literature reviews currently inform research, and how we think they could better do so in the future","author_count":2,"contributors":["Robinson, Raquel","Mekler, Elisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776377"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Literature review","Research synthesis","Rigor","Systematic review","Transparency","Ways of knowing"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"reflections towards more thoughtful engagement with literature reviews in hci","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3686169.3686183","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Funded by the European Union (MSCA-PF, RAGE, 101154545; ERC, THEORYCRAFT, 101043198)."],"id":"S01-000.00-776377","identifier":[{"id":"85215514207","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-1bdc44ec-a22a-42bd-ab9a-5d6eedf3b783"],"keyword":["literature review","research synthesis","rigor","systematic review","transparency","ways of knowing"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-AA7B4EDEB58C081676BACC1A69C0EBD4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3686169.3686183","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Raquel","key":"robinsonpau","last":"Robinson","locid":"cb1b6c5f-6206-4a35-b8aa-6a750f9c89a1","name":"Robinson, Raquel","orcid":"0000-0003-4933-7168","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Elisa","key":"meklerpau","last":"Mekler","locid":"cf6cfa0b-e3ac-4164-8de2-b956d8e23825","name":"Mekler, Elisa","orcid":"0000-0003-0076-6703","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3686169.3686183","status":"p","title":"HttF '24: Proceedings of the Halfway to the Future Symposium"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85215514207"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-1bdc44ec-a22a-42bd-ab9a-5d6eedf3b783"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Reflections Towards More Thoughtful Engagement with Literature Reviews in HCI","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776377","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"author_count":4,"contributors":["Bennett, Daniel","Metatla, Oussama","Roudaut, Anne","Mekler, Elisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-677327"],"filters":{"added":"2023-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Bristol"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"how does hci understand human autonomy and agency","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3544548.3580651","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-677327","identifier":[{"id":"85160004101","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-a9ef6e56-bc41-481d-8686-5faa2b3f7394"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-31050E7723A016E8A2BEF16CB2565EDD"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3544548.3580651","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/98269455/3544548.3580651.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/95236897/CHI_2023_Autonomy.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of bristol","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Bristol","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel","key":"bennettpau","last":"Bennett","name":"Bennett, Daniel","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Oussama","key":"metatlapau","last":"Metatla","name":"Metatla, Oussama","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Anne","key":"roudautpau","last":"Roudaut","name":"Roudaut, Anne","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Elisa D.","key":"meklerpau","last":"Mekler","locid":"cf6cfa0b-e3ac-4164-8de2-b956d8e23825","name":"Mekler, Elisa","name_org":"Mekler, Elisa D.","orcid":"0000-0003-0076-6703","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3544548.3580651","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"CHI '23: CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85160004101"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-a9ef6e56-bc41-481d-8686-5faa2b3f7394"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"How does HCI Understand Human Autonomy and Agency?","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-677327","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"We argue that HCI games research can benefit by understanding experiences of \"jank\". Jank is a gaming vernacular term for game-elements that are somehow “off”: sloppy, glitchy, or clumsy. Examples include frustrating control schemes, primitive visuals, absurd physics errors, and NPCs with wooden, alienating behaviour. Importantly, ‘jank’ is no straightforward term of abuse: players use the term appreciatively, even with affection. Attention to the “paradox” of jank-appreciation can help us understand the limits of usability in understanding player experience. It can clarify conditions which foster reflection and appreciation of adversity, and shed light on games as aesthetic experiences.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Bennett, Daniel","Mekler, Elisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718905"],"filters":{"added":"2024-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Aesthetic experiences in gaming","Game usability","HCI games research","Jank appreciation","Player Experience"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"jank accounts we should study broken games","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101856230/chiplaycompanion23-21.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-718905","identifier":[{"id":"85175851375","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ad67ad80-31e6-419d-ab4f-2645753a6cfd"],"keyword":["Aesthetic experiences in gaming","Game usability","HCI games research","Jank appreciation","Player experience"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B1EEAFFD1ACD25A501952423FD251BCB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101856230/chiplaycompanion23-21.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel","key":"bennettpau","last":"Bennett","name":"Bennett, Daniel","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Elisa","key":"meklerpau","last":"Mekler","locid":"cf6cfa0b-e3ac-4164-8de2-b956d8e23825","name":"Mekler, Elisa","orcid":"0000-0003-0076-6703","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3573382.3616045","status":"p","title":"CHI PLAY Companion '23"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85175851375"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ad67ad80-31e6-419d-ab4f-2645753a6cfd"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Jank Accounts: We Should Study 'Broken' Games","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718905","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Creepy deserted houses should repel visitors, yet they attract excitement of trespassing, dare and social sharing of spooky encounters. Similar engagement is found in game creation systems (GCS) and especially in the online game platform Roblox (Roblox Corporation 2006-). Roblox affords kindred elements for game making and play, especially illustrated in user made creepypasta experiences1; players making creepy games. If creepypastas are regarded as vernacular2 ghost stories of the digital era, are creepypasta games in Roblox the Haunted Houses of the digital era? This presentation extends on an ongoing study asking what makes creepy games appealing, and what conceptual design implications come forth (eg, Byrne et al. 2022 for otherworldly as design resource). By observing selected creepypasta experiences gives grounds to outline a preliminary framework of a Haunted House allegory from three recognized distinctions. An appealing Haunted House (in Roblox) consists of 1) legend-tripping, 2) mythmaking, and questioning if something is 3) potentially true. Haunted Houses and creepy games share kinship in related experiences.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Rautalahti, Heidi","Mekler, Elisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718870"],"filters":{"added":"2024-03","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Finland"],"glorg":["Aalto University","All Global Affiliations"],"keyword":["Creepypasta","Game creation system","Haunted House","Multi player Online Games","Roblox","Vernacular"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"players making creepypastas in roblox kindred appeal of haunted houses and game creation systems","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://digra.org:9998/DiGRA_2023_CR_804.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-718870","ids":["itu-740617b2-87c3-4976-8136-eb3f5ce22c6e"],"keyword":["Creepypasta","Game creation system","Haunted House","Multi-player Online Games","Roblox","Vernacular"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-4ADE2D08FB4AAC847327F0A892D83ACB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://digra.org:9998/DiGRA_2023_CR_804.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"fi","key":"aalto university","loc":["Finland","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Aalto University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Heidi","key":"rautalahtipau","last":"Rautalahti","name":"Rautalahti, Heidi","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Elisa","key":"meklerpau","last":"Mekler","locid":"cf6cfa0b-e3ac-4164-8de2-b956d8e23825","name":"Mekler, Elisa","orcid":"0000-0003-0076-6703","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p","subtitle":"Digital Games Research Association Conference: Limits and Margins of Games","title":"DiGRA '23"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-740617b2-87c3-4976-8136-eb3f5ce22c6e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Players Making Creepypastas in Roblox–Kindred Appeal of Haunted Houses and Game Creation Systems","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718870","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Images are never seen in isolation. Instead, they are perceived within a spatial and temporal tapestry of neighboring images. What impact do other images have on our emotional response toward a particular image? Answers to this basic question have vital implications for a range of fields—especially for visual communication and for curating art, where resources are invested in arranging images within a visual context. Previous studies have provided mixed results, suggesting that juxtaposed images may lead to contrast or assimilation processes increasing and decreasing our liking of an image. But how specific image features in neighboring images (image’s ambiguity or formal similarities between images) modulate our affective interpretation of an image has almost never been explored. In Study 1, we compared the emotion perceived in art photographs (“target” images) when displayed on their own versus when displayed in juxtaposition with negatively or positively valenced nonart (“context”) images. Additionally, we analyzed the influence of the artwork’s perceived ambiguity. In Study 2, we examined the effect of the perceiver’s expertise and the formal similarity between the images on the rated valence of the target image. Our results show that the emotion perceived in the artwork contrasted away from or assimilated toward the valence perceived in the context image depending on which evaluative dimension was activated. Moreover, the influence of negative contextual material on the target image’s valence was more pronounced. We conclude by saying that the evaluative dimension is part of the pictorial context that influences the affective interpretation of an image.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Reymond, Claire","Vornhagen, Jan Benjamin","Pelowski, Matthew","Opwis, Klaus","Mekler, Elisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718880"],"filters":{"added":"2024-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Austria","Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Basel","University of Vienna"],"keyword":["Ambiguity","Context","emotion perceived in art photographs","formal similarity","juxtaposition"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"images influencing images how pictorial context affects the emotional interpretation of art photographs","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101856312/ACA-2021-0815.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-718880","identifier":[{"id":"85158863612","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-86b8b73e-8130-4d49-b190-b366886314a4"],"keyword":["ambiguity","context","emotion perceived in art photographs","formal similarity","juxtaposition"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-432764B76EC51894D2096C4EBA9AFD4F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101856312/ACA-2021-0815.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"ch","key":"university of basel","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Basel","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"at","key":"university of vienna","loc":["Austria","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Vienna","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Claire","key":"reymondpau","last":"Reymond","name":"Reymond, Claire","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Jan Benjamin","key":"vornhagenpau","last":"Vornhagen","locid":"ecc36d3e-77ac-4333-9918-2431807cd4e0","name":"Vornhagen, Jan Benjamin","orcid":"0000-0002-8516-7840","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Matthew","key":"pelowskipau","last":"Pelowski","name":"Pelowski, Matthew","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Klaus","key":"opwispau","last":"Opwis","name":"Opwis, Klaus","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Elisa","key":"meklerpau","last":"Mekler","locid":"cf6cfa0b-e3ac-4164-8de2-b956d8e23825","name":"Mekler, Elisa","orcid":"0000-0003-0076-6703","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1037/aca0000523","issn":["19313896","1931390X"],"journal":"Psychology of Aesthetics, Creativity, and the Arts","publisher":"American Psychological Association","status":"p","title":"Psychology of Aesthetics, Creativity, and the Arts"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105002115479","2-s2.0-85158863612"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-86b8b73e-8130-4d49-b190-b366886314a4"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Images Influencing Images: How Pictorial Context Affects the Emotional Interpretation of Art Photographs","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718880","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Empowering people through technology is a core concern of HCI, yet little is known about how players experience empowerment or disempowerment through videogames. We surveyed 250 participants about their dis-/empowering videogame experiences, investigating why they felt dis-/empowered, and how these experiences related to core player experience constructs including emotion and basic needs satisfaction. While empowering experiences were often positive, and disempowering experiences often negative, we found meaningful exceptions to this, and a surprising complexity in player accounts. We capture this diversity in seven themes. These range from “heroic victories´´ which follow long periods of failure, to positive experiences of disempowerment, which were appreciated for their narrative meaning. By articulating these complex experiences, and relating them to quantitative measures we provide a foundation for understanding of the role of dis-/empowerment in player experience, and highlight avenues for future work. Data and analyses are available at https://osf.io/zhtu8/","author_count":4,"contributors":["Vornhagen, Jan Benjamin","Bennett, Daniel","Murphy, Dooley","Mekler, Elisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718917"],"filters":{"added":"2024-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Emotion in Gaming","Human Computer Interaction","Narrative Meaning in Games","Player Empowerment","Videogame Experience"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"i m the leader and i m going to save the world characterizing empowering and disempowering game experiences","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101397789/DisEmpoweringGamingExperiences_CameraReady.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-718917","identifier":[{"id":"85174421743","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-cbbc0238-dae1-4690-871b-4c7553cdb368"],"keyword":["Emotion in Gaming","Human-Computer Interaction","Narrative Meaning in Games","Player Empowerment","Videogame Experience"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-5193931F7063B7E658FF961932889F13"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101397789/DisEmpoweringGamingExperiences_CameraReady.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"other companies","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-OTH","l3nl":"Other Companies","l3nn":"Other Companies","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Other Companies","namevar":["Laerdal Medical"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-OTH","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jan Benjamin","key":"vornhagenpau","last":"Vornhagen","locid":"ecc36d3e-77ac-4333-9918-2431807cd4e0","name":"Vornhagen, Jan Benjamin","orcid":"0000-0002-8516-7840","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel","key":"bennettpau","last":"Bennett","name":"Bennett, Daniel","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Dooley","key":"murphypau","last":"Murphy","name":"Murphy, Dooley","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Elisa","key":"meklerpau","last":"Mekler","locid":"cf6cfa0b-e3ac-4164-8de2-b956d8e23825","name":"Mekler, Elisa","orcid":"0000-0003-0076-6703","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3611071","status":"p","title":"CHI PLAY 2023"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85174421743"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Other Companies","Private Research","Universities","laerdal medical"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-cbbc0238-dae1-4690-871b-4c7553cdb368"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"“I’m the leader and I’m going to save the world”: Characterizing Empowering and Disempowering Game Experiences","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718917","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Benefits for all user groups is one of the most prominent motivations to provide accessible information and services on the web. Designing digital technologies in a more inclusive manner for users with sensory, motor, or cognitive impairments enhances their overall quality. In practice, work on web accessibility often relies on complying with standards. But whether standards lead to improved usability and a satisfying user experience for all user groups is controversial. The present study aims at deepening our understanding of how compliance with web accessibility standards shapes the experiences of both users with and without disabilities. In a randomised controlled experiment, 66 participants with visual impairments and 65 participants without visual impairments solved tasks on an online shop built with either low (NA) or high (AA) conformance to web accessibility standards. The results show no statistically significant effects on outcomes related to usability and user experience. However, analysis of open-ended answers suggests that participants with visual impairments reported more positive experiences, and participants without visual impairments fewer negative experiences while using the online shop conformant to web accessibility standards. We therefore recommend adopting a more differentiated perspective on what can be achieved through compliance with web accessibility standards and emphasise that conformance-based approaches should be complemented with user-centred and participatory design methods. Further, since most participants reported being experienced users and an online shop is often a familiar context, more research in other settings is required.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Vollenwyder, Beat","Petralito, Serge","Iten, Glena H.","Brühlmann, Florian","Opwis, Klaus","Mekler, Elisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-677322"],"filters":{"added":"2023-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Schwyz University of Teacher Education","University of Applied Sciences and Arts Northwestern Switzerland","University of Basel"],"keyword":["Nondisabled users","Usability","User experience","Users with visual impairments","Web accessibility"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"how compliance with web accessibility standards shapes the experiences of users with and without disabilities","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhcs.2022.102956","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-677322","identifier":[{"id":"85141925827","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-81b552c7-4d97-472a-9c4c-b7496e9cef15"],"keyword":["Nondisabled users","Usability","User experience","Users with visual impairments","Web accessibility"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-8B00EDB55F7CFD860C1ED11244711B59"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhcs.2022.102956","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/107014954/1-s2.0-S1071581922001756-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1071581922001756/pdfft?md5=a540a303cd19b9a55690e5cf3f0d053c&pid=1-s2.0-S1071581922001756-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"ch","key":"university of basel","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Basel","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ch","key":"university of applied sciences and arts northwestern switzerland","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Applied Sciences and Arts Northwestern Switzerland","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"ch","key":"schwyz university of teacher education","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Schwyz University of Teacher Education","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Beat","key":"vollenwyderpau","last":"Vollenwyder","name":"Vollenwyder, Beat","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Serge","key":"petralitopau","last":"Petralito","name":"Petralito, Serge","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Glena H.","key":"itenpau","last":"Iten","name":"Iten, Glena H.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Florian","key":"brühlmannpau","last":"Brühlmann","name":"Brühlmann, Florian","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Klaus","key":"opwispau","last":"Opwis","name":"Opwis, Klaus","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Elisa D.","key":"meklerpau","last":"Mekler","locid":"cf6cfa0b-e3ac-4164-8de2-b956d8e23825","name":"Mekler, Elisa","name_org":"Mekler, Elisa D.","orcid":"0000-0003-0076-6703","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.ijhcs.2022.102956","issn":["10715819","10959300"],"issn_org":["10715819"],"journal":"International Journal of Human-Computer Studies","publisher":"Academic Press","status":"p","title":"International Journal of Human-Computer Studies","volume":"170"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85141925827"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-81b552c7-4d97-472a-9c4c-b7496e9cef15"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"How compliance with web accessibility standards shapes the experiences of users with and without disabilities","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-677322","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Reflection is an important precursor to attitude and behavior change, but existing advice on designing for reflection in games is mixed and requires further empirical investigation. We report on the design and evaluation (n=32) of a game to prompt student reflection on work-life balance. Participants played as themselves or a third person character (Alex). An inductive qualitative analysis of post-play interviews, and a follow-up one week later, resulted in four themes relating to how gameplay facilitated reflection: making (sensible) consequences visible; it's like MY life; the space between Alex and I; and triggers in everyday life. A deductive qualitative analysis also indicated that while both games resulted in different forms of reflection for the majority of players, those who role-played as Alex appeared more likely to experience higher levels of reflection. Through exploring the ways in which the two versions of the game succeeded, and failed, to support reflection, we highlight the importance of providing a relevant context to players (so the game feels close to their experience), and allowing them to role-play as someone other than themselves (but not too close).","author_count":6,"contributors":["Iacovides, Ioanna","Cutting, Joe","Beeston, Jen","Cecchinato, Marta E.","Mekler, Elisa","Cairns, Paul"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381877"],"filters":{"added":"2023-05","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Northumbria University","University of York"],"keyword":["Evaluation","Game design","Reflection","Role play","Work life balance"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"close but not too close distance and relevance in designing games for reflection","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3549487","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381877","identifier":[{"id":"85146328040","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-28b9032b-b396-4512-9694-91dcea9803f0"],"keyword":["evaluation","game design","reflection","role-play","work-life balance"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-386B4E9E63082235CE592FC992668EAF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3549487","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"university of york","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of York","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"northumbria university","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Northumbria University","used":1},{"affno":3,"key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU"}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ioanna","key":"iacovidespau","last":"Iacovides","name":"Iacovides, Ioanna","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Joe","key":"cuttingpau","last":"Cutting","name":"Cutting, Joe","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jen","key":"beestonpau","last":"Beeston","name":"Beeston, Jen","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Marta E.","key":"cecchinatopau","last":"Cecchinato","name":"Cecchinato, Marta E.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Elisa D.","key":"meklerpau","last":"Mekler","locid":"cf6cfa0b-e3ac-4164-8de2-b956d8e23825","name":"Mekler, Elisa","name_org":"Mekler, Elisa D.","orcid":"0000-0003-0076-6703","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paul","key":"cairnspau","last":"Cairns","name":"Cairns, Paul","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3549487","issue":"CHI PLAY","journal":"Proc. ACM Hum. Comput. Interact.","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proc. ACM Hum. Comput. Interact.","title_org":"Proc. ACM Hum.-Comput. Interact.","volume":"6"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-28b9032b-b396-4512-9694-91dcea9803f0"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Close but Not Too Close: Distance and Relevance in Designing Games for Reflection","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381877","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
