, Felix Anand","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Anna","key":"kantosalopau","last":"Kantosalo","name":"Kantosalo, Anna","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nehal","key":"jainpau","last":"Jain","name":"Jain, Nehal","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andrés","key":"luceropau","last":"Lucero","name":"Lucero, Andrés","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Elisa","key":"meklerpau","last":"Mekler","locid":"cf6cfa0b-e3ac-4164-8de2-b956d8e23825","name":"Mekler, Elisa","orcid":"0000-0003-0076-6703","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3491102.3517733","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"CHI '22: CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-03b9fb51-1fba-413c-be03-f8296ae9c221"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Adorned in Memes: Exploring the Adoption of Social Wearables in Nordic Student Culture","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381314","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"The time we each spend using smartphones is increasing. So is the extent of discussions on whether that time is well spent and whether it results in positive experiences and ultimately improves well-being. However, research on this question rarely links the time spent on smartphones, the specific applications used, the motivation for using them and their effects on well-being. We had 70 participants compare experiences with a frequently used smartphone application and an occasionally used one. The participants used the Screen Time feature of the iPhone to select the applications and provided qualitative and quantitative data on their use of the applications. The findings show that the experience of pragmatic and hedonic value differs between the two application types, as does the experience of regret. The motivation for using the applications also in'uences whether the time is experienced as well spent. We use these findings to nuance the general discussion of smartphone usage and well-being.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Rasmussen, Maria A.","Frydendahl, Julie O.","Mekler, Elisa D.","Hornbæk, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-669811"],"filters":{"added":"2023-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Finland"],"glorg":["Aalto University","All Global Affiliations"],"keyword":["Smartphones","User experience","Well being"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"is time on smartphones well spent","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-669811","identifier":[{"id":"85143696893","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-18793844-f8f1-4595-bb5f-cbfe06db6567"],"keyword":["smartphones","user experience","well-being"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6B38B7C5AF660F06B4AFE3267D3E4C72"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"fi","key":"aalto university","loc":["Finland","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Aalto University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Maria A.","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","name":"Rasmussen, Maria A.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Julie O.","key":"frydendahlpau","last":"Frydendahl","name":"Frydendahl, Julie O.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Elisa D.","key":"meklerpau","last":"Mekler","name":"Mekler, Elisa D.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Kasper","key":"hornbækpau","last":"Hornbæk","locid":"148d1e88-e306-46e1-9218-f420470b1ce1","name":"Hornbæk, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0001-8742-1198","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1093/iwc/iwac003","issn":["09535438"],"issue":"5","journal":"Interacting with Computers","pages":"522-536","status":"p","title":"Interacting with Computers","volume":"33"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85143696893"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-18793844-f8f1-4595-bb5f-cbfe06db6567"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Is Time on Smartphones Well Spent?","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-669811","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Videogames receive increasing acclaim as a medium capable of artistic expression, emotional resonance, and even transformative potential. Yet while discussions concerning the status of games as art have a long history in games research, little is known about the player experience (PX) of games as art, their emotional characteristics, and what impact they may have on players. Drawing from Empirical Aesthetics, we surveyed 174 people about whether they had an art experience with videogames and what emotions they experienced. Our findings showcase the prominence of epistemic emotions for videogame art experiences, beyond the negative and mixed emotional responses previously examined, as well as the range of personal impacts such experiences may have. These findings are consistent with art experience phenomena characteristic of other art forms. Moreover, we discuss how our study relates to prior research on emotions and reflection in PX, the importance of games' representational qualities in art experiences, and identify lines of further inquiry.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Bopp, Julia Ayumi","Vornhagen, Jan Benjamin","Mekler, Elisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-669554"],"filters":{"added":"2023-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Finland"],"glorg":["Aalto University","All Global Affiliations"],"keyword":["Art experience","Emotion","Empirical aesthetics","Player Experience","Videogames"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"my soul got a little bit cleaner art experience in videogames","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3474664","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-669554","identifier":[{"id":"85117152256","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-3bb1f86c-f869-47c3-94c0-6b2a7698bcb8"],"keyword":["art experience","emotion","empirical aesthetics","player experience","videogames"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-3B06CBCBA8A4C224E03674E4E87AD223"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3474664","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"fi","key":"aalto university","loc":["Finland","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Aalto University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Julia Ayumi","key":"bopppau","last":"Bopp","name":"Bopp, Julia Ayumi","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jan Benjamin","key":"vornhagenpau","last":"Vornhagen","locid":"ecc36d3e-77ac-4333-9918-2431807cd4e0","name":"Vornhagen, Jan Benjamin","orcid":"0000-0002-8516-7840","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Elisa D.","key":"meklerpau","last":"Mekler","locid":"cf6cfa0b-e3ac-4164-8de2-b956d8e23825","name":"Mekler, Elisa","name_org":"Mekler, Elisa D.","orcid":"0000-0003-0076-6703","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3474664","issue":"CHI PLAY","journal":"Proc. ACM Hum. Comput. Interact.","pages":"1-19","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proc. ACM Hum. Comput. Interact.","volume":"5"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-3bb1f86c-f869-47c3-94c0-6b2a7698bcb8"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"\"My Soul Got a Little Bit Cleaner\": Art Experience in Videogames","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-669554","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"author_count":2,"contributors":["Tyack, April","Mekler, Elisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-669555"],"filters":{"added":"2023-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"off peak an examination of ordinary player experience","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3411764.3445230","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-669555","identifier":[{"id":"85106707287","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ce16c7fc-031a-41bc-b194-87520a4bc540"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-FE0DF24BCCB48F54FE40C48E24B3531B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3411764.3445230","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3411764.3445230","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"April","key":"tyackpau","last":"Tyack","name":"Tyack, April","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Elisa D.","key":"meklerpau","last":"Mekler","locid":"cf6cfa0b-e3ac-4164-8de2-b956d8e23825","name":"Mekler, Elisa","name_org":"Mekler, Elisa D.","orcid":"0000-0003-0076-6703","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3411764.3445230","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"CHI '21: CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ce16c7fc-031a-41bc-b194-87520a4bc540"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Off-Peak: An Examination of Ordinary Player Experience","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-669555","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Digital games have evolved into a medium that moves beyond basic toys for distraction and pleasure towards platforms capable of and effective at instigating more serious, emotional, and intrapersonal experiences. Along with this evolution, games research has also started to consider more deeply affective and cognitive reactions that resemble the broad notion of eudaimonia, with work already being done in communication studies and media psychology as well as in human–computer interaction. These studies offer a large variety of concepts to describe such eudaimonic reactions—including eudaimonia, meaningfulness, appreciation, and self-transcendence—which are frequently used as synonyms as they represent aspects not captured by the traditional hedonic focus on enjoyment. However, these concepts are potentially confusing to work with as they might represent phenomenological distinct experiences. In this scoping review, we survey 82 publications to identify different concepts used in digital gaming research to represent eudaimonia and map out how these concepts relate to each other. The results of this scoping review revealed four broad conceptual patterns: (1) appreciation as an overarching (yet imprecise) eudaimonic outcome of playing digital games; (2) covariation among meaningful, emotionally moving/challenging, and self-reflective experiences; (3) the unique potential of digital games to afford eudaimonic social connectedness; and (4) other eudaimonia-related concepts (e.g., nostalgia, well-being, elevation). This review provides a conceptual map of the current research landscape on eudaimonic game entertainment experiences and outlines recommendations for future scholarship, including how a focus on digital games contributes to a more comprehensive understanding of eudaimonic media experiences broadly.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Daneels, Rowan","Bowman, Nicholas","Possler, Daniel","Mekler, Elisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-669552"],"filters":{"added":"2023-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Belgium","Germany","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Hanover University of Music, Drama, and Media","Texas Tech University","University of Antwerp"],"keyword":["Appreciation","Digital Games","Eudaimonia","Meaningfulness","Self reflection","Social connectedness","emotional challenge","emotionally moved","media entertainment"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the eudaimonic experience a scoping review of the concept in digital games research","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.17645/mac.v9i2.3824","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-669552","identifier":[{"id":"85105741640","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-1d0fd3e4-87b0-4968-b7cf-45201a1ef006"],"keyword":["appreciation","digital games","emotional challenge","emotionally moved","eudaimonia","meaningfulness","media entertainment","self-reflection","social connectedness"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-DBE8502536CA20C5405036208C6346EE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.17645/mac.v9i2.3824","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"be","key":"university of antwerp","loc":["Belgium","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Antwerp","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"texas tech university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Texas Tech University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"hanover university of music drama and media","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Hanover University of Music, Drama, and Media","u